Z||000000||Pheagey||11-05-2001||12:02 PM||||   Ok, how hard is this to do? My car's sitting on the side of the road (locked) and I need to get it moving ASAP but on a limited budget.<BR>   Also, anyone ever try to get the moonroof off from the outside? Pointers please...<P>------------------<BR>"There is no replacement for displacement.Period"<BR>    -Mr.Frosty<P>'88 coupe, 5 speed<BR>Broken down: 3 times<BR>Broken into: 1 time<BR>Killed: '97 Civic LX Auto,<BR>Killed by: '93 Civic EX 5 speed,<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||11-05-2001||01:10 PM||||So, you've locked your keys in the car and need to know how to get it?  I have heard, but never tried, that if you bang the sunroof near the latch, or the roof near the latch, it will sometimes pop open, and you can then reach in and push the little buttons to take it all the way out.  Then, you can unlock your doors.<P>If you've lost your keys, I don't know how to take the ignition lock cylinder out, because when I replaced mine (wouldnt go into ACC), you had to put the key it to turn it all the way around and then pull it out.<P>Door lock cylinders are an ever larger PITA, they are in the most un-accessable area of the door.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||11-05-2001||03:03 PM||||Getting the ignition lock cylinder out isn't too hard.  You have to remove the steering wheel (need puller) and the lock plate underneath it.  (A lock plate removal tool makes this MUCH easier)  Then the turn signal switch has to be removed and put out of the way.  1 screw holds the cylinder in.  You can remove it in the "lock" position without a key.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||11-05-2001||04:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodrv6:<BR><B>Getting the ignition lock cylinder out isn't too hard.  You have to remove the steering wheel (need puller) and the lock plate underneath it.  (A lock plate removal tool makes this MUCH easier)  Then the turn signal switch has to be removed and put out of the way.  1 screw holds the cylinder in.  You can remove it in the "lock" position without a key.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just changed mine a couple weeks ago. That's exactly how I did it. Wasn't hard at all, just took a little time. I think it took me an hour and a half.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">              <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
